          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "You should seek care from your healthcare provider regarding your diagnosis of type 2 diabetes.\n\nA diagnosis of type 2 diabetes means that your body is not effectively using insulin, which leads to higher-than-normal blood glucose levels. This condition typically develops gradually and may often go unnoticed until significant health issues arise. Proper management is crucial to prevent serious complications associated with prolonged high blood sugar levels. (Source: \"Type 2 diabetes mellitus\")\n\nFor first-line therapy, current guidelines recommend metformin as the initial treatment, supplemented with structured lifestyle interventions. Metformin is preferred because of its proven effectiveness, affordability, and minimal risk of causing low blood sugar when used alone. The choice of medication may also depend on any existing health conditions you may have. (Source: \"Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management\")\n\nIn terms of coding, type 2 diabetes is classified under the ICD-10 code E11. This code is used for billing and documentation purposes related to this condition. (Source: \"Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management\")\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
